  "result": "Alright, I'll take that liberty.\n\n### The Color Namer\n\nWalter Finch has named 4,318 colors in thirty-seven years at the Bureau of Standardization.\n\nHe works on the ninth floor of a grey building downtown. The eighth floor is Tax, the tenth is Forms. The ninth is the only floor with no windows that open. The blinds are always half-down, so the light comes in in stripes.\n\nOn his desk there is a thermos, a magnifying glass, a spectroscope no one else knows how to use, and two notebooks. The one on the right is official. The one on the left has a cracked spine and an elastic band holding it shut.\n\nHis official names have to be two words. A proper adjective and a proper noun. *Harbor Fog. Brushed Nickel. Late August.* They are filed, stamped, fed into a computer, and eventually printed on the back of paint cans and crayon boxes.\n\nWalter is color-blind. Reds and greens run together for him like two rivers. He can tell a color by its brightness, by whether it feels warm or cold in the hand, but he cannot *see* it the way you can.\n\nThis, he tells people, makes him good at the job. People who can see color trust their eyes. He has to trust what the color *means*.\n\nSamples arrive in sealed envelopes with a field note. A lab in Oregon will mix something by accident and send the swatch. A geologist will scrape lichen off a cliff face. A deep-sea submersible will photograph a fish no one has ever seen alive, and the lab will try to reproduce its skin.\n\nWalter reads the note first. He never looks at the swatch first.\n\n*Collected from the interior of a beached piano, Oregon coast. Smells faintly of salt and old hymns.*\n\nHe holds the paper up to the blinds. He thinks about salt. He thinks about hymns. He writes: **Piano Tide.**\n\nIn the left notebook, he writes what it really is: *The color of the inside of a piano nobody plays anymore, but that someone loved enough to leave by the sea.*\n\nPriya, the intern, started in March. She\u2019s 24, sharp, and convinced the ninth floor is a prank played on new employees. On her third day she leaned over his desk and said, \"Sir, with respect, why do we name them at all? A color is a color. You could just call it #4471.\"\n\nWalter took a sip of his tea \u2014 always too much sugar \u2014 and said, \"If we don\u2019t name it, we\u2019ll forget it was ever seen.\"\n\nShe laughed, but she started reading his left notebook when she thought he wasn\u2019t looking.\n\nHis private names are longer. They are not allowed. They are the color of a mood, not a material.\n\n*Bruise on a Peach.*\n*Grandmother\u2019s Kitchen Tile, 1968.*\n*Grief, But Not Quite.*\n*The Exact Blue of a Train Ticket to Somewhere You Never Went.*\n\nOn Friday, his last Friday, a last envelope arrived. Sample #4472. No field note. Just a slip of paper, a little curled, the color of everything and nothing. A soft, pale grey with a hint of yellow in it, like light through a dirty window.\n\nWalter held it up. It was bright in the middle and tired at the edges. It wasn\u2019t a color from a cliff or a lab. It was a color from a morning.\n\nHe sat for a long time. The blinds made stripes across the back of his hand. He could hear the kettle in the break room click off.\n\nHe wrote the official name first, because that was the job: **Kettle Light.**\n\nThen he opened the left notebook, on the last clean page, and wrote the true one:\n\n*Morning, Still Asleep. The color of the kitchen at 6:14 a.m. when Elsie got up first. Before she made noise. Before she put the kettle on. Just the light, deciding whether to stay.*\n\nHe put the swatch in the notebook and left it on the desk for whoever got the ninth floor next. He took his thermos home and washed it in the sink. It had been Elsie\u2019s, once. He kept using it because the lid still fit right.\n\nPriya found the notebook on Monday. She didn\u2019t file it. She put it in her bag.\n\nThat afternoon, she held #4472 up to her own window, which did open, and for the first time she understood that Walter hadn\u2019t been naming colors at all.\n\nHe\u2019d been keeping them.",
